{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790496412,"nonce":"1PuwdRB7tr+iruwqqRYLzqdxjn0=","title":"LLVM: ordered compares of unsigned integers used signed predicates","message":"llvm_backend.rs emitted SLT/SLE/SGT/SGE for every HIR Binary Lt/Le/Gt/Ge whatever its type, so on the LLVM tier an unsigned compare of values with the top bit set (u64::MAX \u003e 1) took the signed answer (false). Cranelift and the HIR interpreter already compared by the operand type. It mattered once 3b8d6479 lowered Python's list index check to one unsigned compare: a negative position would have passed the check on the LLVM tier. Fixed in 3b8d6479 (ULT/ULE/UGT/UGE for U8 to U128); regression test crates/compiler/tests/unsigned_compare.rs runs x\u003ey, x\u003e=y, x\u003cy, x\u003c=y on u64::MAX and 1 on the interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM.","files":null}]}